read-string-does-not-skip-comments — real AutoCAD/BricsCAD readers are stricter about comments than clautolisp's; line comments are --bricscad-specific, block comments break BOTH vendors
==========================================================================================================================================================================================

Status: OPEN, root cause fully characterized (2026-09-08) — two
distinct failure modes now confirmed on real hardware, across both
vendors and platforms. Found while building an unrelated project
(autocad-gui-prototype, loader.lsp), which has since worked around it
by not calling `read` on a string at all (see Downstream fix below).

Summary
-------

  (read ";; foo\nbar")            -- LINE comment before the object

    BricsCAD V26 (real, macOS)    -> NIL
    BricsCAD V25 (real, Windows)  -> NIL   (same behaviour, x-platform)
    AutoCAD 2022 (real, Windows)  -> BAR   (skips it, like load)
    clautolisp (any dialect)     -> BAR

  (read ";|foo|; bar")            -- BLOCK comment before the object

    BricsCAD (V25/V26, both)     -> NIL
    AutoCAD 2022 (real, Windows)  -> NIL   (does NOT skip a block comment)
    clautolisp (any dialect)     -> BAR

So this is TWO separate findings, not one:

  - Line comments (`;` to end of line): a genuine, pure `--dialect
    bricscad` divergence. AutoCAD's reader matches clautolisp's own
    (lenient) behaviour here; only BricsCAD returns NIL.
  - Block comments (`;|...|;`): NOT bricscad-specific — BOTH real
    vendors tested return NIL, and only clautolisp is lenient. This is
    clautolisp being too permissive relative to EVERY real product
    tested, not a --bricscad-only gap.

In both cases the failure mode is the same shape: the reader returns
NIL the instant a comment (of either kind) is the first non-blank
thing it meets, before any real object — leading whitespace/newlines
before the comment or the object don't matter either way. Comments
trailing AFTER a first object, or interspersed between multiple
objects in one string across repeated `read` calls, are NOT
characterized here — narrower, separate question if it ever matters
to a caller.

Original repro (autocad-gui-prototype/loader.lsp): a small loader read
a metadata file (a foo.alpm, itself starting with four `;;;;` header-
comment lines, then a real `(alpm-define-system ...)` form) via
read-line + strcat (reconstructing the whole file into one string) and
a single `(read whole-string)` on the result. Under clautolisp this
returned the expected form every time headless — 130/130 tests green,
including an end-to-end reload of loader.lsp. On real BricsCAD V26 it
silently returned NIL, every single time, for all three files affected
(each starting with the same four-line comment header) — plain
`load`-based file loads elsewhere in the exact same project were
completely unaffected (they never call `read` on a string, only `load`
on a file). Confirmed via a live BricsCAD probe: the reconstructed
string was verified byte-correct (exact length, exact content,
comments included) via `strlen`/`princ`, then `(read that-string)`
still came back NIL. Root cause pinned down only after several other
hypotheses (SECURELOAD/TRUSTEDPATHS, boundp reliability on `C:`
symbols, literal-path identity, `foreach` vs. plain function nesting,
file encoding) were each tested live on BricsCAD and ruled out first.

Fix
---

Two separate fixes, matching the two separate findings above:

  - Line comments: a narrow, `--bricscad`-dialect-scoped change. Under
    `--bricscad`, `read` (string form) should return NIL on a leading
    `;`-line-comment, matching real BricsCAD exactly. Under `--autocad`
    and `--clautolisp`, KEEP the current skip-and-continue behaviour —
    it already matches real AutoCAD 2022, and clautolisp's own dialect
    has no vendor to match, so leniency there is a deliberate
    convenience, not a bug.
  - Block comments: NOT dialect-scoped — clautolisp is too lenient
    relative to BOTH real vendors tested, so `read` returning NIL on a
    leading `;|...|;` block comment is arguably the correct default
    behaviour under `--autocad` AND `--bricscad`, and worth considering
    even under `--clautolisp` (own dialect) for consistency, unless the
    lenient block-comment handling there is a deliberate, documented
    convenience someone is relying on — needs a decision, not just a
    mechanical port of the bricscad fix.
  - Either way, this is exactly the kind of per-dialect (for line
    comments) / general-correctness (for block comments) gap
    deferred-clautolisp-out-of-dialect-warnings.issue's mechanism could
    eventually flag — but that issue's mechanism is about a
    MISSING-operator warning (READ exists in every dialect here, so it
    wouldn't fire as designed today). If the fixes above land as real
    behaviour changes, a warning becomes largely moot for --bricscad
    (it would just be correct) — but ANY caller relying on the current
    lenient comment-skipping in `read`-on-a-string (there may be other
    code out there doing what autocad-gui-prototype's loader.lsp
    originally did) would silently start getting NIL instead of an
    object once either fix lands. Worth a one-time NEWS/changelog
    callout when shipped, even without the general warning mechanism.
  - Check the autolisp-spec pages for READ against both findings —
    does the spec text say anything about comment-skipping being a
    LOAD/file-reader responsibility rather than READ's own? It should
    end up documenting both divergences explicitly (bricscad vs.
    autocad/clautolisp for line comments; clautolisp vs. both real
    vendors for block comments), not left silent or generic either way.

Downstream fix (autocad-gui-prototype, not clautolisp itself)
---------------------------------------------------------------

Two iterations, worth recording since the second one is the actual
lesson: a first pass (commit bfc057d) hand-rolled a character-by-character,
string-literal-aware comment stripper (both line and block forms) to
run before `read`. Working, but reimplementing a chunk of a Lisp
reader's tokenizer downstream is exactly the kind of thing worth
avoiding if there's a better option. There was: `alpm-define-system`
(~/src/ogamita/alpm/sources/alpm.lsp) is a real function, invoked by
ordinary evaluation when a .alpm file is `load`ed — the .alpm's own
top-level form is a function call, not inert data, once that name is
bound. Final fix (commit 309c5f2): a minimal local stub for
`alpm-define-system` (captures its `def` argument, none of the real
system's validation/registry machinery — no dependency on the real
ALPM package) installed before `load`ing the .alpm file directly.
`load` has never shown either comment-handling gap on any product
tested, so this sidesteps both findings above entirely rather than
trying to re-derive the exact vendor rules downstream. See
loader.lsp's own `fs-loader--alpm-plist`.

Found by
--------

Original macOS/BricsCAD V26 repro and root-cause isolation:
autocad-gui-prototype/loader.lsp live-BricsCAD debugging session,
2026-09-08 (Claude Sonnet 5, with pjb driving BricsCAD interactively).
Cross-platform/cross-vendor confirmation for BOTH the line-comment case
(BricsCAD V25 Windows matches V26 macOS; AutoCAD 2022 Windows matches
clautolisp instead) and the block-comment case (AutoCAD 2022 also
returns NIL, unlike its own line-comment behaviour) supplied directly
by pjb the same day, from separate own testing/knowledge — not
re-derived via a live probe in this session.

Related: deferred-clautolisp-out-of-dialect-warnings.issue (the
general per-dialect-behavior warning mechanism, discussed above as a
partial alternative/fallback to the direct fixes).
